      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "One of the two great language-and-peoples branches in this story (the other being Celtic). The Germanic peoples lived beyond Rome's northern frontier and spoke an early form of the family that later produced English, Dutch, German, and the Scandinavian tongues. The Angles, Saxons, and Jutes who crossed into Britain were Germanic — and their language, not the Britons' Celtic one, became English."
